;; Docs for std/net.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "net.bjodoc"). The texts
;; are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "TCP connections, each as a pair of byte ports: an InputPort and an OutputPort.")
  (see tcp-connect tcp-listen accept-evt accept close-listener! peer-address)
  (example """
    (import (std net))
    (import (std ports))

    ;; A client: ask, end the write half, read the answer.
    (match (tcp-connect "127.0.0.1" 8080)
      ((Ok (Tuple in out))
       (write-bytes! out request)
       (flush-port out)
       (shutdown! out)              ; a FIN: "I have finished asking"
       (read-some in))              ; the answer still arrives
      ((Err e) None))
    """)
  (example """
    ;; A server: accept until the listener is closed.
    (: serve (-bjo-> Listener Symbol))
    (defbjo (serve l)
      (let go ()
        (match (sync (choose (wrap (accept-evt l) Some)
                             (wrap (listener-closed l) (fun (_) None))))
          ((Some (Ok conn)) (spawn (handle conn)) (go))
          ((Some (Err e))   (log (str "accept failed: " (.-Message e))) 'failed)
          (None             'stopped))))
    """)
  (reference """
    A connection is a @code{(Tuple InputPort OutputPort)} and nothing more.
    There is no socket object, which is why this module is
    @code{(std net)} and not @code{(std socket)}. A protocol written against
    @code{(std ports)} runs over TCP and does not know that it is TCP, and a
    @code{byte-pipe} can replace the network in a test.

    Everything you do with a connection comes from @code{(std ports)} and
    the prelude: @code{read-some}, @code{read-bytes-evt},
    @code{write-bytes!}, @code{flush-port}, @code{shutdown!},
    @code{limited}, and @code{read-line} on the same input port as
    @code{read-bytes}. Import both modules.

    @strong{The socket goes when both halves are closed}, or when the owner
    of the connection releases it, whichever comes first. So
    @code{(with-open ((i in) (o out)) ...)} closes the socket at its end.
    Closing one half does not close the other: "I have finished listening,
    now I answer" must work.

    @strong{Owners.} A connection from @code{tcp-connect} or
    @code{stream->ports} is owned by the ambient scope. A connection from an
    accept is owned by its listener, and the listener is owned by the scope
    in which @code{tcp-listen} was called. When that scope ends, it closes
    the listening socket and every accepted connection that is still open.

    @strong{The rule that accepts obey.} A connection that the operating
    system has given over belongs to the listener, not to the event that
    asked for it. An accept never takes a connection from the kernel. It
    takes one from the slot of the listener, and only when it wins. Thus
    @code{accept-evt} is a real CML event: a branch of a @code{choose} that
    loses accepts nothing, and the connection stays for the next acceptor.

    Nagle's algorithm is off on every socket. @code{flush-port} sends what
    is buffered at once.

    @code{BjolangRuntime/BjoNet.cs} has the implementation.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Connecting
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc tcp-connect
  (summary "Connects to host on port, and returns the two ports of the connection.")
  (arg host "An IP address, such as @code{\"127.0.0.1\"} or @code{\"::1\"}, or a host name.")
  (arg port "The TCP port, from 0 to 65535.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok (Tuple in out))}, or @code{(Err e)} when the connection fails, the name does not resolve, or port is out of range.")
  (raises Bjolang.Runtime.ScopeEnded "When there is no ambient scope. This happens before a socket exists.")
  (see tcp-listen stream->ports peer-address shutdown!)
  (example """
    (with-deadline 5000
      (tcp-connect "example.com" 80))
    """)
  (reference """
    A host name is resolved, and its addresses are tried in order. The
    first address that answers is used. There is no "happy eyeballs" race.
    An address literal does not use the resolver.

    The connection is owned by the ambient scope. If the program does not
    close it, the scope closes it when the scope ends.

    There is no @code{#:deadline}. The connect watches the ambient
    cancellation token, so @code{with-deadline} and a cancelled scope stop
    it. A deadline or a cancellation is a raise and not an @code{Err}, and
    no other address is tried after it.

    Nagle's algorithm is off on the socket. A byte port has its own write
    buffer, and @code{flush-port} means "send this now". With Nagle on, a
    small write after a flush would wait for an ACK, which is wrong for a
    request/response protocol.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; A connection somebody else made
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc stream->ports
  (summary "The two ports over a duplex Stream that some other code connected.")
  (arg s "The stream, for example a @code{NetworkStream}, or the stream an HTTP server gives after @code{101 Switching Protocols}.")
  (key peer "What @code{peer-address} returns for the input port. @code{\"\"}, the default, gives @code{None}.")
  (returns "The input port and the output port, which share the stream.")
  (raises Bjolang.Runtime.ScopeEnded "When there is no ambient scope.")
  (see tcp-connect peer-address stream->input-port stream->output-port)
  (reference """
    @code{stream->input-port} and @code{stream->output-port} each own the
    stream that they wrap. Thus the first half that is closed would take
    the stream from the other half. This function shares the stream as
    @code{tcp-connect} does: the stream is disposed when both halves are
    closed, or when the ambient scope ends.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Listening
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Listener
  (summary "A listening TCP socket whose accepts are CML events.")
  (see tcp-listen accept-evt accept close-listener! listener-closed))

(:doc tcp-listen
  (summary "Binds a TCP socket to host and port, and listens on it.")
  (arg host "An IP address to bind, such as @code{\"0.0.0.0\"} or @code{\"::1\"}, or a host name.")
  (arg port "The TCP port, from 0 to 65535. With 0, the operating system picks a port; @code{listener-port} tells which.")
  (key backlog "How many connections the kernel holds before an accept takes them. At least 1.")
  (returns "@code{(Ok listener)}, or @code{(Err e)} when the bind fails, the name does not resolve, or an argument is out of range.")
  (raises Bjolang.Runtime.ScopeEnded "When there is no ambient scope.")
  (see accept-evt accept listener-port close-listener!)
  (example """
    (match (tcp-listen "127.0.0.1" 0)
      ((Ok l) (log (str "listening on " (int->string (listener-port l)))))
      ((Err e) (log (.-Message e))))
    """)
  (reference """
    The listener is owned by the ambient scope. When the scope ends, it
    does the @strong{hard} stop: it closes the listening socket and every
    accepted connection that is still open. @code{close-listener!} is the
    graceful stop.

    A host name is resolved synchronously, because a bind happens once, at
    startup. Only the first address of the name is used. An address
    literal does not use the resolver.
    """))

(:doc listener-port
  (summary "The port that the listener is bound to.")
  (arg l "The listener.")
  (returns "The bound port. Useful when @code{tcp-listen} was given 0.")
  (see tcp-listen)
  (reference """
    It is still available after the listener is closed.
    """))

(:doc accept-evt
  (summary "The event that accepts the next connection on the listener.")
  (arg l "The listener.")
  (returns "An event whose value is @code{(Ok (Tuple in out))}, or @code{(Err e)} when the listening socket has failed.")
  (see accept listener-closed close-listener! tcp-listen)
  (reference """
    This is a real CML event. If another branch of a @code{choose} wins,
    nothing is accepted, and the connection stays for the next acceptor.
    The event is also @strong{ephemeral}: each @code{sync} asks the
    listener again. Thus a server loop accepts one connection after
    another, and does not win with the same connection each time.

    The listener holds at most one connection that the kernel has given
    over and no accept has taken. The kernel's backlog is the queue behind
    it.

    @strong{Why the value is a @code{Result}.} A listening socket can fail,
    and the CML resume path carries a value, not a raise. Thus a failure
    that comes while a fiber waits in a @code{choose} must arrive as a
    value. @code{promise-join}, @code{blocking} and the byte reads of
    @code{(std ports)} return a @code{Result} for the same reason.

    @strong{A failure is permanent.} After the first failure, the listener
    accepts nothing more, and every later @code{sync} on this event
    commits at once with the same @code{Err}. A loop that logs the error
    and accepts again does not stop. Stop the loop, or make a new listener.

    On a closed listener, the event never commits. Choose it together with
    @code{listener-closed}, as @code{inbox-recv} is chosen with
    @code{inbox-closed}. After the scope that owns the listener has ended,
    a @code{sync} on this event raises @code{ObjectDisposedException}.

    The connection belongs to the listener. The listener's scope closes it
    when it ends, if both halves are not closed before. A connection that
    is accepted at the moment the listener closes is not tracked by the
    listener; its two ports are then the only way to close it.
    """))

(:doc listener-closed
  (summary "The event that fires when the listener has stopped accepting.")
  (arg l "The listener.")
  (returns "An event that fires once, and then commits at once on every @code{sync}.")
  (see close-listener! accept-evt)
  (reference """
    It fires on @code{close-listener!}, and when the scope that owns the
    listener ends.
    """))

(:doc accept
  (summary "Waits for the next connection on the listener and returns its two ports.")
  (arg l "The listener.")
  (returns "The input port and the output port of the connection.")
  (raises System.Net.Sockets.SocketException "When the listening socket has failed. The failure of the socket is raised as it is.")
  (see accept-evt listener-closed)
  (reference """
    This syncs @code{accept-evt}, and raises the error of an @code{Err} on
    the caller's stack. In a bjoroutine it suspends; in an ordinary
    function it parks the thread.

    On a closed listener it waits forever, because the event never
    commits. A program that can meet a closed listener uses
    @code{accept-evt} in a @code{choose} with @code{listener-closed}.
    """))

(:doc close-listener!
  (summary "Stops accepting connections. Connections already accepted stay open.")
  (arg l "The listener.")
  (see listener-closed tcp-listen)
  (reference """
    This is the @strong{graceful} stop, and that is the difference from
    the end of the listener's scope. The listening socket is closed, and
    @code{listener-closed} fires. An accept that waits never commits. A
    connection that the kernel gave over but that no accept took is
    closed.

    Connections that were already accepted are @strong{not} touched. They
    belong to the code that handles them, and "stop listening" does not
    mean "stop a request that is half answered". This is what
    @code{inbox-close!} means for an inbox.

    So a server drains like this: close the listener, let the handlers
    finish, and leave the scope. The scope then closes what is still open.

    A second call does nothing.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Who is at the other end
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc peer-address
  (summary "The address and port of the other end of a connection, for a log line.")
  (arg p "The input port of a connection.")
  (returns "@code{(Some address)}, for example @code{\"127.0.0.1:54321\"}, or @code{None} when the port has no peer.")
  (see tcp-connect accept-evt stream->ports)
  (reference """
    The peer is kept with the input port, because the tuple has two places
    and there is nowhere else to keep it.

    It is @code{None} for a port that is not the input port of a
    connection: a file, a pipe or a @code{limited} view. It is also
    @code{None} for @code{stream->ports} with no @code{#:peer}.
    """))
